ral features like TimestampNormalizer, ComposeNormalizers, and ExpectedFiles that don't appear to have test coverage yet, even though the test file covers basic matching and divergence scenarios.\n\nLooking at the source code, I can see requirement IDs scattered throughout—R1.1-R1.4 in the DiffTest struct, R2.1-R2.6 in runBinary and buildEnv, R3.1-R3.6 in comparison logic, R4.1-R4.4 in normalization, and R5.1-R5.2 in ExpectedFiles. So I could propose a task to add tests for those normalization requirements specifically.\n\nThe task would involve creating tests for the normalization functions—testing TimestampNormalizer with various formats, testing ComposeNormalizers to verify multiple functions compose correctly, and testing applyNormalizers with both nil and non-nil function lists. This is well-scoped and directly tied to R4.1-R4.4.\n\nBut I'm wondering whether I should be adding tests to existing packages or if there's something else I should prioritize first.\n\nLooking at the release hierarchy, rel00.0 has all code but some tests may be incomplete, while rel02.0 and rel02.1-02.2 are still pending specs. The earliest concrete work I can identify is filling test coverage gaps in rel00.0 packages.\n\nActually, testutils normalization tests make sense because they're in the earliest release, have clear acceptance criteria tied to specific requirement IDs, and the test file currently doesn't cover these functions. But I should verify whether adding tests for already-working code is really what the pipeline needs right now.\n\nGiven that the project's success criteria emphasize 100% output parity against the Homebrew reference binary, comprehensive test coverage for these core normalization functions is critical since they're used by all differential tests. So I'll focus on testing the normalization functions in prd001-testutils R4.1-R4.4.\n\nLet me double-check the code status to make sure this is the right priority...\n\nThe pipeline tracking shows \"not started\" 