I'm scoping this to just those requirements and leaving the SIGPIPE test out since it's complex to implement reliably in a test environment. Test that version binary prints version string to stdout and exits 0 (prd011-magefiles R1.1)\n- R2: Test that default version is \"dev\" when ldflags are not set (prd011-magefiles R1.2)\n- R3: Test that version binary built with custom -ldflags prints the custom version string (prd011-magefiles R1.2)\n- R4: Test that --version and -v flags produce the same output (prd011-magefiles R1.4)\n\nThat's 4 task requirements mapped to 3 PRD sub-requirements. That's OK per P6.\n\nAcceptance criteria:\n- AC1: go test ./cmd/version/... passes\n- AC2: Default binary prints \"dev\\n\" to stdout\n- AC3: Binary built with -ldflags \"-X main.version=v1.2.3\" prints \"v1.2.3\\n\" to stdout\n- AC4: --version and -v flags produce identical output to no-flag invocation\n\nDesign decisions:\n- D1: Use TestMain to build the binary into a temp directory, following the pattern from cmd/cat/cat_test.go\n- D2: No reference binary needed — version is a project-specific utility, not a GNU reimplementation\n- D3: Test two build configurations: default (no ldflags) and custom version (with ldflags)\n\nNow I'm confirming the test file naming convention matches the project pattern — it should be version_test.go, consistent with cat_test.go, du_test.go, ls_test.go, and sponge_test.go. , wc_test.go. So the pattern is the utility name + _test.go. For version: version_test.go.\n\nBut cmd/version only has main.go and no other Go files. The test file would be version_test.go in package main.\n\nOK, let me finalize:\n\nTitle: \"Test cmd/version prd011-magefiles R1.1, R1.2, R1.4\"\n\nRequired reading:\n- cmd/version/main.go\n- docs/specs/product-requirements/prd011-magefiles.yaml (R1 requirements)\n- cmd/cat/cat_test.go (TestMain pattern for building binary)\n\nFiles:\n- cmd/version/version_test.go\n  action: create\n  note: Tests for version binary output and exit code\n\nI need to verify the version binary prints the correct output with a newline and exits cleanly, handles the default \"dev\" version when no linker flags are set, respects custom versions passed via -ldflags, and ensures both --version and -v flags work identically to running without flags. I'll follow the TestMain pattern from the cat tests to build the binary in a temporary directory, and since version is a standalone utility rather than a GNU reimplementation, I won't need reference binaries or differential testing. I'm planning to build two separate binaries to test both the default and custom version scenarios. I need to make sure the task is self-contained for the stitch agent — it needs to know what to build, how to test it, what the expected behavior should be, and what patterns to follow from the existing codebase. Let me verify my design decisions are grounded in the PRD and architecture rather than invented, since the constraint is clear on that point.\n\nThe TestMain pattern comes from existing project code, which feels like a reasonable convention to point to even if it's not explicitly in the PRD. The observation about no reference binary is just a factual note to prevent the agent from looking for something that doesn't exist. And the two build variants are directly derived from the requirement about default values when ldflags aren't set, so that's clearly PRD-grounded.\n\nNow I'm checking the line count against the constraint of 150-250 lines per task. Looking at comparable test files in the project, they're all in the 240-260 range with 7-8 test functions. For version_test.go, I'd expect around 90 lines total across TestMain, a few basic test cases, and some helpers. That's well under the target, but it makes sense given how simple the version utility is—I shouldn't artificially pad it just to hit a number.\n\nThe bigger issue is that I'm only creating one file when the constraint asks for 5-7 files. But again, for a straightforward test task,