ttern in Go projects when testing concerns are logically distinct, even if they're in the same source file. For the title, I need to include the specific requirement IDs being tested.\n\nThinking through what's actually testable: R4.1 is just a type definition, but I can verify that functions properly conform to the NormalizeFunc signature. R4.3 covers sequential application through applyNormalizers, which I can exercise indirectly via ComposeNormalizers since it applies functions in order. So testing ComposeNormalizers effectively covers both the sequential application requirement and the composition requirement. I should include all four requirements in the test title since they're all being validated. I'm settling on four test requirements that cover the core functionality: TimestampNormalizer handling syslog, ISO, and time-only formats; preserving non-timestamp text; and ComposeNormalizers chaining functions in sequence. The acceptance criteria confirm these tests pass, the normalizer handles all three timestamp patterns, composition applies functions in order, and non-timestamp content stays unchanged.\n\nFor the implementation approach, I'm using an external test package consistent with the existing difftest_test.go pattern, creating a separate normalize_test.go file to keep concerns isolated, and structuring tests as table-driven subtests with parallelization. Though I'm reconsidering whether to add to the existing difftest_test.go instead—the file naming convention suggests test files should mirror their source, but that file is already substantial at 247 lines, and the constraint limits each task to at most 4 PRs, so a focused separate file makes more sense. I'm realizing that Go allows multiple test files for a single source file, so creating a separate normalize_test.go is perfectly valid and keeps things organized. The P7 rule about test file naming just establishes the convention for the main test file, not that additional test files can't exist. I'll go ahead with creating normalize_test.go to test the normalization functions without bloating the existing difftest_test.go file, then write out the final task definition.\n\nNow I'm mapping out what needs to be tested and which files I'll need to reference — the existing difftest.go and difftest_test.go to understand the patterns, plus the product requirements document to ensure I'm covering all the normalization rules. I'm organizing the test structure by creating a separate normalize_test.go file to keep concerns isolated, and I'll follow the existing patterns with table-driven subtests and parallel execution. The acceptance criteria confirm that all three timestamp patterns need to be handled correctly and that function composition should work as expected.\n\nNow I'm checking the YAML structure to make sure it's valid and reviewing against the P9 guideline for task scope. I have 4 requirements which is slightly below the 5-8 target range, so I should consider whether I need to add another requirement to better balance the task.\n\nLooking at what I could test, I could add a requirement for TimestampNormalizer handling mixed content on the same line, but I need to be careful not to inflate the count by over-splitting R4.2 which is already covered by my existing requirements. Since the guideline calls for 5-8 requirements and I'm at 4, adding one more requirement would bring me to the target range. I'll add a requirement for testing TimestampNormalizer with lines containing both timestamp and non-timestamp text.\n\nNow I'm refining the requirements list with specific test cases mapped to prd001-testutils R4.2, defining what each test should validate.\n\nI'm noticing a potential issue with the mapping constraint—I have multiple task requirements derived from a single PRD requirement (R4.2), which might violate the \"exactly one\" mapping rule. Let me reconsider whether these should be consolidated or if the constraint allows for one PRD requirement to decompose into