{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01V9snN8P9qhfTgVpJx2UKgU","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"## Project State Summary\n\n**Problem**: go-unix-utils is an experiment in specification-driven development. It derives formal specs from C source code of Unix utilities (coreutils, moreutils, grep, findutils), generates Go implementations from those specs using an AI orchestrator, and verifies parity via differential testing against GNU reference binaries.\n\n**Architecture**: Four-stage pipeline (extract → specify → synthesize → verify). Key components: `cmd/` (per-utility Go implementations), `pkg/testutils/` (differential testing harness), `pkg/sys/` (Darwin/Linux syscall abstractions), `pkg/format/` (output formatting), `magefiles/` (build targets, scaffolded externally), `docs/` (human-authored specs).\n\n**Current state**: Source code exists for all spec-complete packages: `pkg/testutils`, `pkg/sys`, `pkg/format`, `cmd/version`, `cmd/cat`, `cmd/wc`, `cmd/sponge`, `cmd/du`, `cmd/ls`. The next releases (rel02.0 through rel02.2) covering trivial utilities (true, false, yes, basename, dirname, tee, head, seq) have **pending** spec status — PRDs haven't been written yet, which is human-operator work that blocks code generation.\n\n**Priority reasoning**: Releases 00.0 through 01.3 and 03.0 through 04.1 all have source code present (trusted over stale code_status prose). Releases 02.0–02.2 need human-authored PRDs first. The highest-priority gap I can identify within existing spec-complete packages is **test coverage for `pkg/testutils` normalization functions** (R4.2–R4.4). `TimestampNormalizer` and `ComposeNormalizers` are exported functions with zero direct test coverage. These are foundational infrastructure in rel00.0 and will be used by `cmd/ts` and other timestamp-producing utilities.\n\n```yaml\n- index: 0\n  title: \"Test prd001-testutils R4.2, R4.3, R4.4 normalization functions\"\n  dependency: -1\n  description: |\n    deliverable_type: code\n\n    required_reading:\n      - pkg/testutils/difftest.go (TimestampNormalizer, ComposeNormalizers, NormalizeFunc type, timestampPatterns regexps, timestampPlaceholder constant)\n      - pkg/testutils/difftest_test.go (existing test patterns -- external test package testutils_test, table-driven subtests with t.Parallel, helper binary compilation in TestMain)\n      - docs/specs/product-requirements/prd001-testutils.yaml (R4 normalization requirements)\n\n    files:\n      - path: pkg/testutils/normalize_test.go\n        action: create\n        note: Tests for TimestampNormalizer and ComposeNormalizers exported functions\n\n    requirements:\n      - id: R1\n        text: \"Test TimestampNormalizer replaces all three timestamp pattern types -- syslog (\\\"Feb 19 12:34:56\\\"), ISO (\\\"2024-02-19 12:34:56\\\"), and time-only (\\\"12:34:56\\\") -- with the fixed placeholder string, and preserves text that contains no timestamp patterns, per prd001-testutils R4.2\"\n      - id: R2\n        text: \"Test that ComposeNormalizers applies normalizer functions in sequence -- output of the first function feeds as input to the second -- per prd001-testutils R4.3\"\n      - id: R3\n        text: \"Test ComposeNormalizers returns a single NormalizeFunc that chains the given functions and handles edge cases (zero functions returns data unchanged, single function behaves identically to calling that function directly) per prd001-testutils R4.4\"\n\n    design_decisions:\n      - id: D1\n        text: \"Use external test package testutils_test consistent with difftest_test.go\"\n      - id: D2\n        text: \"Create normalize_test.go as a separate test file -- both _test.go files in pkg/testutils/ are compiled together by go test but separation keeps each file focused on its area\"\n      - id: D3\n        text: \"Use table-driven subtests with t.Parallel() following the pattern established in difftest_test.go and other test files in the project\"\n\n    acceptance_criteria:\n      - id: AC1\n        text: \"go test ./pkg/testutils/... passes with all new tests\"\n      - id: AC2\n        text: \"TimestampNormalizer replaces syslog format (e.g. \\\"Feb 19 12:34:56\\\") with \\\"<TIMESTAMP>\\\"\"\n      - id: AC3\n        text: \"TimestampNormalizer replaces ISO format (e.g. \\\"2024-02-19 12:34:56\\\") with \\\"<TIMESTAMP>\\\"\"\n      - id: AC4\n        text: \"TimestampNormalizer replaces time-only format (e.g. \\\"12:34:56\\\") with \\\"<TIMESTAMP>\\\"\"\n      - id: AC5\n        text: \"Text containing no timestamp patterns is returned unchanged by TimestampNormalizer\"\n      - id: AC6\n        text: \"ComposeNormalizers(f, g)(data) applies f first then g, verified with two functions that produce distinct observable transformations\"\n      - id: AC7\n        text: \"ComposeNormalizers with zero functions returns input data unchanged\"\n```"}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":83931,"cache_read_input_tokens":19289,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":83931},"output_tokens":9,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"b0ef8e51-8229-4dc2-9a8e-b17e41cc3169","uuid":"171b91b9-a885-4d41-b83b-6fd56450a84b"}
